.TH CCOL_CIRCULAR_QUEUE_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-26" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_circular_queue_destroy \- destroy a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_circular_queue_destroy(cq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro frees all the resources of the queue and then sets the queue
pointer to NULL.

ccol_event_loop_remove() does not wait for a callback that it already
gave to a reactor thread, so such a callback can be inside this queue
when the removal returns. This call blocks until every one of those
callbacks finishes with the queue, and only then frees the queue. This
is what makes the documented teardown sequence (drain, remove, destroy)
safe as written.

A callback that the loop runs for a different registration can run this
whole sequence for this queue, with any num_reactor_threads. A dispatch
that the loop collected for it but did not start yet does not hold the
destroy up, and it never runs its callback after the removal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue to destroy. The macro sets it to NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
You can call this with a NULL pointer.

The macro evaluates cq exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so an expression with a
side effect, such as handles[i++], destroys and clears one element.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Messages that stay in the queue cause an assertion failure, so drain
the queue before you destroy it. The destroy counts the messages only
after every callback that the removal left running has finished, and a
write callback that was already running when ccol_event_loop_remove()
returned can send, so that message causes the assertion failure too.
When a write registration watches the queue, remove it, wait for its
on_removed callback, and only then drain and destroy the queue.
A watcher on this queue also causes an assertion failure. A watcher is a
ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an ccol_event_loop
registration (ccol_selectable_from_circq or ccol_selectable_from_chan).
Without that assertion, the waiter would stay linked and point to the
mutex of this queue, which the destroy is about to free.
Call ccol_event_loop_remove() before you destroy the queue, or let
every ccol_select() call and every ccol_select_timed() call that
watches the queue return first.
Do not call this from inside an ccol_event_loop callback that dispatches
this queue: the destroy would wait for that same callback. This is a
caller error, and the program stops through ccol_fatal_err().
Do not hold an application lock across this call if one of these
callbacks takes that lock, because the destroy waits for the callback
while the callback waits for the lock.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circular_queue_create (3)
